CORRECTION: My first StructuredOutput call was wrong — it claimed the files do not exist. That was an error caused by incomplete `find` invocations; in fact I successfully Read the cited file in my very first tool call. The package and file ARE present at /Users/petepetrash/Code/aui/assistant-ui/.claude/worktrees/tw-glass-updates/packages/tw-glass/src/index.css and the disk searches later confirmed it. I am re-verifying the actual claim here.

WHAT THE CODE ACTUALLY SHOWS (index.css lines 164-170, generator generate.mjs lines 240-243):
@utility glass-text {
  color: transparent;
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
  background-clip: text;
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
}
So the factual core of the claim is CONFIRMED: `color: transparent` is set unconditionally with no @supports guard, alongside both `-webkit-background-clip: text` and `background-clip: text`. There is no @supports anywhere in the package (grep across index.css, generate.mjs, README found none).

WHERE THE CLAIM OVERSTATES (why partial, not confirmed at medium):

1. Browser-compat reality is much weaker than the claim implies. `background-clip: text` (and the `-webkit-` prefixed form) has been supported in ALL major engines for years: Chrome/Edge (Blink) via -webkit- since ~2014, Safari (WebKit) via -webkit- since ~2014, and Firefox supports BOTH `-webkit-background-clip: text` AND unprefixed `background-clip: text` since Firefox 49 (2016). The claim's framing — "The standard (unprefixed) background-clip:text is comparatively recent in Firefox" — is misleading: the component ships the `-webkit-` prefixed line too (line 166), and Firefox has honored `-webkit-background-clip: text` for ~9 years. Because the prefixed declaration comes first and the unprefixed second, any engine that supports either keeps text visible. There is effectively no current/recent mainstream browser (within the Tailwind v4 / baseline-modern support window this package targets) where text-level background-clip is unsupported but the page otherwise renders. The "invisible glyphs" scenario requires an engine with no -webkit-background-clip:text AND no background-clip:text support, which in 2026 means only very old/exotic engines.

2. Real-world user impact is therefore low, not medium. The first-fold practical risk (truly invisible text) is the OTHER, separate misuse case the evidence itself acknowledges ("independent of the missing-background misuse case") — i.e., applying glass-text without a background-image, which is a usage/docs issue, not this @supports issue.

3. The recommendation (wrap color:transparent in @supports ((-webkit-background-clip: text) or (background-clip: text))) is a legitimate, cheap defensive hardening and is reasonable to adopt. But it guards against a near-empty set of real browsers, so it is polish/robustness, not a medium-severity correctness fix.

NET: The literal code observation (unconditional color:transparent, no @supports) is accurate. The severity and the browser-compat justification are overstated. Downgrading to low.
